added_lemmas.statement:
Every embedded law \(P_{K,\theta}^{\mathrm{emb}}\) satisfies the independent and identically distributed Bernoulli-design setup, compact-domain conditions, and continuous-objective condition of the anchor identification result, and \(L(P_{K,\theta}^{\mathrm{emb}})=L_K\).  These laws need not obey any \(K\)-uniform smoothness or later rate hypothesis.  For every sequence of anchor-containing classes \(\mathfrak P_K\), if \(\lambda_K\to\lambda\in[0,\infty)\), then
\[
 \liminf_{K\to\infty}R_K^{\mathrm{anc}}(\mathfrak P_K)\ge R(\lambda),
 \qquad
 \liminf_{K\to\infty}C_{K,\alpha}^{\mathrm{anc}}(\mathfrak P_K)
 \ge C_\alpha(\lambda).                           \tag{46}
\]
If instead \(\lambda_K\to\infty\), then, for every \(\rho_0\in(-r,r)\) and every family of broader local classes satisfying (45),
\[
 \lim_{T\to\infty}\lim_{H\to\infty}\liminf_{K\to\infty}
 \inf_{D\in\mathscr D_K^{\mathrm{anc}}}
 \sup_{P\in\mathfrak B_K(\rho_0,H)}
 \mathbb E_P\ell_T\!\left(\sqrt{\lambda_K}\{D-L(P)\}\right)
 \ge V(\rho_0).                                   \tag{47}
\]
These are converse-only transfers: they assert neither collision-based upper bounds nor minimax equalities over \(\mathfrak P_K\) or \(\mathfrak B_K(\rho_0,H)\).

added_lemmas.proof_tex:
Let \(\iota_K(j)=j/K\).  This is a measurable bijection from \(\{1,\ldots,K\}\) to its image in \([0,1]\), with measurable inverse on that finite image.  Under \(P_{K,\theta}^{\mathrm{emb}}\), the covariate is uniform on this image, the conditional outcome law in arm \(d\) is \(\operatorname{Bernoulli}(p_{dj})\) at \(z=\iota_K(j)\), and the fixed-count arm samples are independent.  Equivalently, before conditioning on the arm counts, one may generate independent and identically distributed units with a Bernoulli treatment indicator of any fixed probability strictly between zero and one, independent of the covariate and the two potential outcomes, and set the observed response equal to the potential outcome in the assigned arm.  Thus consistency, randomization, and overlap hold.  The domains \([0,1]\) are compact, \(\{0,1\}\subset[0,1]\), and
\[
 h(y_0,y_1)=|y_1-y_0|
\]
is continuous.  Hence the hypotheses of Lemma \(\mathrm{lem{:}anchor\mbox{-}sharp\mbox{-}cot\mbox{-}identification}\) hold.  Applying that cited anchor result identifies (41) as the causal sharp lower endpoint.  Proposition \(\mathrm{prop{:}sharp\mbox{-}cot\mbox{-}endpoint}\), whose coupling proof is cellwise and invariant under the relabeling \(\iota_K\), then gives
\[
 L(P_{K,\theta}^{\mathrm{emb}})=L_K=a-b\rho_K.     \tag{48}
\]
No later uniform smoothness premise follows from this embedding.  For example, choose signs alternating between adjacent cells in either arm.  Then adjacent conditional means differ by \(2a\) or \(2b\) across covariate distance \(1/K\), so every Lipschitz constant for that conditional mean is at least \(2aK\) or \(2bK\).  Thus the embedded family has no \(K\)-uniform Lipschitz bound; moreover its binary outcome laws are atomic rather than positive Lebesgue densities.  This proves the asserted limitation without imposing a new restriction.

We next use the subclass monotonicity technique.  Take any \(D\in\mathscr D_K^{\mathrm{anc}}\).  Restrict it to observations whose covariates lie in \(\iota_K(\{1,\ldots,K\})\), apply \(\iota_K^{-1}\) coordinatewise, and clip its action to \(\mathcal D=[a-b,a+b]\).  The result is a member \(D_K\in\mathscr D_K\).  Since (48) lies in \(\mathcal A\subset\mathcal D\), clipping cannot increase absolute loss.  The inclusion defining \(\mathfrak P_K\) therefore yields, for every such \(D\),
\[
 \sup_{P\in\mathfrak P_K}\mathbb E_P|D-L(P)|
 \ge \sup_{\theta\in\mathcal T_K(r)}
      \mathbb E_\theta|D_K-L_K|
 \ge R_K.                                         \tag{49}
\]
Taking the infimum over \(D\) preserves (49), so
\[
 R_K^{\mathrm{anc}}(\mathfrak P_K)\ge R_K.        \tag{50}
\]
If \(\lambda_K\to\lambda<\infty\), Theorem \(\mathrm{thm{:}finite\mbox{-}information\mbox{-}transfer}\) gives \(R_K\to R(\lambda)\); taking lower limits in (50) proves the first inequality in (46).

Now take a rule \(\mathcal C\in\mathscr C_K^{\mathrm{anc}}\) satisfying the anchor-class coverage constraint (44).  On the embedded support, relabel by \(\iota_K^{-1}\), intersect its reported interval with \(\mathcal A\), and, if the intersection is empty, report the fixed singleton \(\{a\}\).  Because every embedded target (48) belongs to \(\mathcal A\), this operation preserves coverage of that target and cannot increase length.  It therefore produces a finite-array rule \(\mathcal C_K\in\mathscr C_K\) satisfying
\[
 \inf_{\theta\in\mathcal T_K(r)}
 \Pr_\theta\{L_K\in\mathcal C_K\}\ge1-\alpha,  \tag{51}
\]
and
\[
 \sup_{P\in\mathfrak P_K}\mathbb E_P\operatorname{len}(\mathcal C)
 \ge \sup_{\theta\in\mathcal T_K(r)}
      \mathbb E_\theta\operatorname{len}(\mathcal C_K)
 \ge C_{K,\alpha}.                                \tag{52}
\]
Infimizing (52) over every rule satisfying (44) gives
\[
 C_{K,\alpha}^{\mathrm{anc}}(\mathfrak P_K)\ge C_{K,\alpha}. \tag{53}
\]
Theorem \(\mathrm{thm{:}honest\mbox{-}interval\mbox{-}transfer}\) gives \(C_{K,\alpha}\to C_\alpha(\lambda)\), so (53) proves the second inequality in (46).

Finally suppose \(\lambda_K\to\infty\), fix \(T,H\), and take \(D\in\mathscr D_K^{\mathrm{anc}}\).  Restriction, relabeling, and clipping as above give \(D_K\in\mathscr D_K\).  Since \(u\mapsto\ell_T(u)\) is nondecreasing in \(|u|\), clipping cannot increase the displayed loss on an embedded target.  The inclusion (45) and (48) imply
\[
 \begin{aligned}
 &\sup_{P\in\mathfrak B_K(\rho_0,H)}
 \mathbb E_P\ell_T\!\left(\sqrt{\lambda_K}\{D-L(P)\}\right)\\
 &\qquad\ge
 \sup_{\theta\in\mathcal H_K(\rho_0,H)}
 \mathbb E_\theta\ell_T\!\left(\sqrt{\lambda_K}(D_K-L_K)\right).
                                                               \tag{54}
 \end{aligned}
\]
Taking the infimum over anchor rules on the left and observing that their restricted rules form a subset of \(\mathscr D_K\) gives
\[
 \inf_{D\in\mathscr D_K^{\mathrm{anc}}}
 \sup_{P\in\mathfrak B_K(\rho_0,H)}\mathbb E_P\ell_T(\cdots)
 \ge
 \inf_{D_K\in\mathscr D_K}
 \sup_{\theta\in\mathcal H_K(\rho_0,H)}
 \mathbb E_\theta\ell_T\!\left(\sqrt{\lambda_K}(D_K-L_K)\right). \tag{55}
\]
Apply, in the stated order, \(\liminf_{K\to\infty}\), then \(\lim_{H\to\infty}\), and then \(\lim_{T\to\infty}\).  Theorem \(\mathrm{thm{:}deterministic\mbox{-}shell\mbox{-}local\mbox{-}minimax}\) identifies the ordered limit on the right of (55) as \(V(\rho_0)\), proving (47).  Since every step was a restriction to a contained submodel, no upper procedure or equality has been transferred to either broader class.

proposed.construction:
An anchor observable law \(P\) is generated by independent and identically distributed units carrying a covariate \(Z\in[0,1]\) and potential outcomes \((Y(0),Y(1))\in[0,1]^2\).  A Bernoulli treatment indicator, with success probability strictly between zero and one, is independent of \((Z,Y(0),Y(1))\), and the observed outcome obeys consistency, \(Y=Y(d)\) in arm \(d\).  Conditional on the realized arm sizes \(n_0,n_1\), its observation \(\mathcal O_K^{\mathrm{anc}}\) is the corresponding pair of independent within-arm samples.  Write \(\mu_{d,z}^P\) for the conditional observed-outcome law in arm \(d\) at covariate value \(z\), and define the observed-law conditional-transport functional
\[
 L(P)=\int_{[0,1]}
 \inf_{\gamma\in\Pi(\mu_{0,z}^P,\mu_{1,z}^P)}
 \int_{[0,1]^2}|y_1-y_0|\,d\gamma(y_0,y_1)\,dP_Z(z).
 \tag{41}
\]
The covariate and outcome domains are compact and the cost \(h(y_0,y_1)=|y_1-y_0|\) is continuous.

For \(\theta\in\mathcal T_K(r)\), let \(P_{K,\theta}^{\mathrm{emb}}\) be the law obtained from \(\mathcal F_K\) by the injective relabeling \(j\mapsto z_j=j/K\), regarding \(Y\in\{0,1\}\) as taking values in \([0,1]\).  An anchor-containing class \(\mathfrak P_K\) is any class of anchor observable laws which contains \(P_{K,\theta}^{\mathrm{emb}}\) for every \(\theta\in\mathcal T_K(r)\).  Let \(\mathscr D_K^{\mathrm{anc}}\) be all randomized measurable kernels from \(\mathcal O_K^{\mathrm{anc}}\) to \([0,1]\), and let \(\mathscr C_K^{\mathrm{anc}}\) be all randomized measurable kernels from \(\mathcal O_K^{\mathrm{anc}}\) to the closed subintervals of \([0,1]\).  Define
\[
 R_K^{\mathrm{anc}}(\mathfrak P_K)
 =\inf_{D\in\mathscr D_K^{\mathrm{anc}}}
   \sup_{P\in\mathfrak P_K}\mathbb E_P|D-L(P)|,  \tag{42}
\]
and
\[
 C_{K,\alpha}^{\mathrm{anc}}(\mathfrak P_K)
 =\inf_{\mathcal C\in\mathscr C_K^{\mathrm{anc}}}
   \sup_{P\in\mathfrak P_K}\mathbb E_P\operatorname{len}(\mathcal C), \tag{43}
\]
where the infimum in (43) is subject to
\[
 \inf_{P\in\mathfrak P_K}P\{L(P)\in\mathcal C\}\ge1-\alpha. \tag{44}
\]
For \(\rho_0\in(-r,r)\) and \(H>0\), a broader local class \(\mathfrak B_K(\rho_0,H)\) is any class of anchor observable laws satisfying
\[
 \{P_{K,\theta}^{\mathrm{emb}}:
   \theta\in\mathcal H_K(\rho_0,H)\}
 \subseteq\mathfrak B_K(\rho_0,H).                \tag{45}
\]
No smoothness or rate condition beyond the displayed anchor conditions is included in this definition.
